dxgi: Signal a video memory budget event as it is registered.

Windows signals the event passed to
IDXGIAdapter3::RegisterVideoMemoryBudgetChangeNotificationEvent once at
registration, and DXVK's DXGI does the same ("This method seems to fire the
event immediately on Windows"). Red Dead Redemption 2 depends on it: after it
has tried out its Direct3D 12 devices it registers an event and, on its main
thread, waits for it without a timeout before it makes its window. With the
registration only noted, the game sat with no window until the Rockstar
launcher was closed.

No budget change is reported after that one signal, as before.

LOCAL TO THIS PORT, NOT FOR DXMT. Applied by macos/build-dxmt.sh to
github.com/athei/dxmt at 01e59e9bde030f105937f249afaac92ada01b4d7. Written
with an AI assistant (Claude). DXMT does not accept contributions made or
co-authored by AI (its CONTRIBUTING.md), so this patch must not be sent to it
as a pull request. What was found may be reported to its developers, for them
to write their own fix; this patch leaves here when the pinned commit has one.
---
diff --git a/src/dxgi/dxgi_adapter.cpp b/src/dxgi/dxgi_adapter.cpp
index 7ae5b54..cb18761 100644
--- a/src/dxgi/dxgi_adapter.cpp
+++ b/src/dxgi/dxgi_adapter.cpp
@@ -274,6 +274,8 @@ public:
     if (!event || !cookie)
       return DXGI_ERROR_INVALID_CALL;
     *cookie = 0;
+    // Windows signals the event once as it is registered, and a game may wait for that
+    SetEvent(event);
     return S_OK;
   }
 
